# Everything is LF, including PowerShell.
#
# This used to declare `*.ps1 text eol=crlf`, and the pre-commit
# mixed-line-ending hook forces LF on every staged file. The two disagreed, and
# every commit that touched a .ps1 file became a fix-then-re-add loop: the hook
# rewrote the file, git re-checked it out as CRLF on the next checkout, and the
# next commit repeated it. It surfaced as `mixed line ending ... Failed` on a
# commit whose files were already LF, which is a confusing way to learn that a
# config file and a hook are fighting.
#
# LF is also the safer choice here, and not only for tidiness. A CRLF script
# handed to busybox dies with `syntax error: unexpected end of file`, which this
# project already hit; several attack scripts build shell fragments in a
# PowerShell here-string and hand them to a container. LF removes that hazard
# rather than managing it.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.vhdx binary
*.tar binary
*.gz binary
